    "Paths are relative to this folder. Every track is CC0 1.0; licenses/<id>.txt holds the licence statement read on its source page; licenses/CC0-1.0.txt is the legal code.",
    "Each file is a 100-105 s excerpt from the recording's first sound (excerpt.fromSeconds; leading silence over 0.25 s is skipped), ending on a bar start with a 2 s fade, AAC-LC 160 kb/s, linear gain toward referenceLufs (never past truePeakCeiling).",
    "Length: every track covers the longest video from start point 0 (durationSeconds - startPoint >= video + 2 s): sources are at most 95 s and the edit only shortens them. No loop or fallback is needed for coverage.",
    "integratedLufs/truePeak/lra: ffmpeg ebur128 on the bundled .m4a. loudestSection: the 10 s span with the highest mean short-term loudness.",
    "bpm/beatSeconds/barSeconds/firstDownbeatSeconds: beat-grid estimate (onset autocorrelation, grid fit on full-band + kick-band flux, 4/4 assumed; beats land up to ~20 ms early). The metrical level comes from the curator's tempo: bpm is the best grid within 15% of it, so it cannot flip to double, half or 2/3 tempo (provenance.json keeps the unconstrained estimate as freeBpm).",
    "downbeat: which beat starts the bar. agreed = two cues pick the same beat: the whole-track kick-and-harmony pattern, and a kick-like first strong onset on the grid. estimated = the pattern alone; bar starts, phrase start points and the excerpt end may then be one to three beats off. Start point 0 is exact either way.",
    "startPoints: 0 (the recording's start) and the estimated 4-bar phrase starts (on or up to ~20 ms before the beat) that leave at least 20 s. introSeconds: first start point where the full arrangement is playing (within 3 LU of the median short-term loudness).",
    "loops (informational; not used to pick a track): [fromSeconds, toSeconds) spans whose next 2 bars match the 2 bars after fromSeconds; jumping from toSeconds back to fromSeconds continues the music.",
    "moods/energy/character are the curator's tags. A track is picked by the plan's mood, then its energy, then length, then deterministically by job."
